{"task": "Prove exactly this local conditional obligation using the supplied NL route. Return only the tactic body through submit_transition. All displayed parameter and input proof binders are already in context.", "packet": {"version": 2, "contract_sha256": "b899a2e0303ad3cf09f48c7a036fe5e600316bb0c158bc8f3498f0372a6cd0ed", "header": "import Mathlib
open MeasureTheory Set Filter
open scoped BigOperators ContDiff
noncomputable section
namespace WaveMap
abbrev Space (d : ℕ) := EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)
def positive (n : ℕ) : Set (Space n × ℝ) := {p | 0 < p.2}
def box (n : ℕ) (R : ℝ) : Set (Space n) := {x | ∀ j, x j ∈ Icc (-R) R}
def openBox (n : ℕ) (R : ℝ) : Set (Space n) := {x | ∀ j, x j ∈ Ioo (-R) R}
def timeInterval (t : ℝ) : Set ℝ := Icc (t / 2) (3 * t / 2)
def timeInterior (t : ℝ) : Set ℝ := Ioo (t / 2) (3 * t / 2)
def spatial {n : ℕ} {F : Type} [NormedAddCommGroup F] [NormedSpace ℝ F]
    (f : Space n → F) (j : Fin n) (x : Space n) : F :=
  fderiv ℝ f x (PiLp.single 2 j 1)
def ut {n m : ℕ} (u : Space n × ℝ → Space m) (x : Space n) (t : ℝ) : Space m :=
  deriv (fun r => u (x, r)) t
def utt {n m : ℕ} (u : Space n × ℝ → Space m) (x : Space n) (t : ℝ) : Space m :=
  deriv (ut u x) t
def ux {n m : ℕ} (u : Space n × ℝ → Space m) (j : Fin n) (x : Space n) (t : ℝ) : Space m :=
  spatial (fun y => u (y, t)) j x
def uxx {n m : ℕ} (u : Space n × ℝ → Space m) (j : Fin n) (x : Space n) (t : ℝ) : Space m :=
  spatial (fun y => ux u j y t) j x
def utx {n m : ℕ} (u : Space n × ℝ → Space m) (j : Fin n) (x : Space n) (t : ℝ) : Space m :=
  spatial (fun y => ut u y t) j x
def laplace {n m : ℕ} (u : Space n × ℝ → Space m) (x : Space n) (t : ℝ) : Space m :=
  ∑ j, uxx u j x t
def density {n m : ℕ} (u : Space n × ℝ → Space m) (x : Space n) (t : ℝ) : ℝ :=
  ‖ut u x t‖ ^ 2 + ∑ j, ‖ux u j x t‖ ^ 2
def densityTime {n m : ℕ} (u : Space n × ℝ → Space m) (x : Space n) (t : ℝ) : ℝ :=
  deriv (density u x) t
def energy {n m : ℕ} (u : Space n × ℝ → Space m) (t : ℝ) : ℝ :=
  ∫ x, density u x t
def kineticProduct {n m : ℕ} (u : Space n × ℝ → Space m) (t : ℝ) (x : Space n) : ℝ :=
  inner ℝ (ut u x t) (utt u x t)
def gradientProduct {n m : ℕ} (u : Space n × ℝ → Space m) (j : Fin n) (t : ℝ) (x : Space n) : ℝ :=
  inner ℝ (ux u j x t) (utx u j x t)
def laplaceProduct {n m : ℕ} (u : Space n × ℝ → Space m) (j : Fin n) (t : ℝ) (x : Space n) : ℝ :=
  inner ℝ (ut u x t) (uxx u j x t)
def flux {n m : ℕ} (u : Space n × ℝ → Space m) (j : Fin n) (t : ℝ) (x : Space n) : ℝ :=
  inner ℝ (ut u x t) (ux u j x t)
def terms {n m : ℕ} (u : Space n × ℝ → Space m) (t : ℝ) (j : Fin n) : List (Space n → ℝ) :=
  [fun x => density u x t, fun x => densityTime u x t, kineticProduct u t,
   gradientProduct u j t, laplaceProduct u j t, flux u j t, spatial (flux u j t) j]
def quotient {n m : ℕ} (u : Space n × ℝ → Space m) (t h : ℝ) (x : Space n) : ℝ :=
  (density u x (t + h) - density u x t) / h
def bound (n : ℕ) (R B : ℝ) : Space n → ℝ :=
  (box n R).indicator (fun _ => B)
end WaveMap
", "parameters": [{"name": "n", "type": "ℕ"}, {"name": "m", "type": "ℕ"}, {"name": "u", "type": "WaveMap.Space n × ℝ → WaveMap.Space m"}, {"name": "q", "type": "WaveMap.Space m"}], "representation": "Use one ambient Euclidean representation throughout: Space d = EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d) = PiLp 2 (fun _ => ℝ). Its norm and real inner product are the Euclidean ones; PiLp.inner_apply identifies the latter with the coordinate dot product. Fin n labels the original n coordinates by j.val + 1; it introduces neither a missing endpoint nor an extra coordinate. PiLp.single 2 j 1 is the unit vector in coordinate j, so spatial is the Fréchet derivative evaluated on that vector. ut and utt are successive ordinary time derivatives, uxx successive same-coordinate spatial derivatives, and utx is the spatial derivative of ut. Under H002 equality of mixed derivatives identifies utx with the time derivative of ux; proving this identification is part of T004/T007, not a hidden assumption. densityTime is the ACTUAL time derivative of the density, not an assumed product formula. The parameter u is an arbitrary total ambient representative; H002 and all geometric hypotheses concern only positive times. Any source map extends by zero for t≤0, without requiring boundary regularity, and restriction recovers it. Since positive times form an open set, derivatives of all orders and energy germs there depend only on that restriction. No conclusion concerns the chosen exterior values. ContDiffOn uses (∞ : ℕ∞ω), meaning every finite derivative order, not ω (analyticity) or the top element of ℕ∞ω. Header definitions assert no regularity or integrability. The total Bochner integral in energy gains its intended finite Lebesgue meaning only after T003 proves integrability. volume is standard normalized Euclidean Lebesgue measure; EuclideanSpace.volume_preserving_symm_measurableEquiv_toLp identifies it with product coordinate Lebesgue measure. In N002, iteratedFDeriv of positive natural order is the multilinear encoding of all mixed space/time derivatives: evaluation on coordinate unit vectors recovers them, and multilinearity plus the coordinate basis gives the converse. All this is in the same Euclidean representation; local product-coordinate measure transport in T008 is checked inside that obligation. No M ports are retained: the four supplied library theorems are used directly under their actual hypotheses. H003 is preserved although the source proof never needs the norm of q. For measures, ENNReal.ofReal ((2*R)^n) encodes a nonnegative real box volume; R>0 and the explicit non-top clause prevent treating an infinite mass as a real finite integral. Integrable means almost everywhere strong measurability together with finite integral of the norm. The supplied dominated-convergence theorem requires these norm and measurability conditions, not merely pointwise convergence. For the scalar FTC, ℝ supplies completeness; the derivative must exist on the unordered closed interval and its function must be interval integrable. Fubini will be used with finite restricted coordinate measures, hence s-finite, and a genuinely integrable real function. These typeclass capabilities are properties of the chosen standard spaces and measures, not additional hypotheses on the wave map.", "inputs": [{"id": "H001", "binder": "h_H001", "type": "1 ≤ n ∧ 1 ≤ m"}, {"id": "H002", "binder": "h_H002", "type": "ContDiffOn ℝ (∞ : ℕ∞ω) u (WaveMap.positive n)"}, {"id": "T002", "binder": "h_T002", "type": "∀ t0 : ℝ, 0 < t0 → ∃ (C : Set (WaveMap.Space n)) (R : ℝ),
  IsCompact C ∧ 0 < R ∧ C ⊆ WaveMap.openBox n R ∧
  (∀ x : WaveMap.Space n, x ∉ C → ∀ r ∈ WaveMap.timeInterval t0, u (x,r) = q) ∧
  (∀ (k : ℕ) (x : WaveMap.Space n) (r : ℝ), 0 < k → x ∉ C →
    r ∈ WaveMap.timeInterior t0 → iteratedFDeriv ℝ k u (x,r) = 0)"}], "target": "(∀ t : ℝ, 0 < t → Integrable (fun x => WaveMap.density u x t)) ∧
  ContDiffOn ℝ (∞ : ℕ∞ω) (fun p => WaveMap.density u p.1 p.2) (WaveMap.positive n) ∧
  ContDiffOn ℝ (∞ : ℕ∞ω) (fun p => WaveMap.densityTime u p.1 p.2) (WaveMap.positive n) ∧
  ∀ t0 : ℝ, 0 < t0 → ∃ (C : Set (WaveMap.Space n)) (R η B : ℝ),
    IsCompact C ∧ 0 < R ∧ 0 < η ∧ 0 ≤ B ∧
    C ⊆ WaveMap.openBox n R ∧ IsCompact (WaveMap.box n R) ∧
    volume (WaveMap.box n R) = ENNReal.ofReal ((2 * R) ^ n) ∧
    volume (WaveMap.box n R) ≠ ⊤ ∧
    Icc (t0 - η) (t0 + η) ⊆ WaveMap.timeInterior t0 ∧
    (∀ x : WaveMap.Space n, x ∉ C → ∀ r ∈ WaveMap.timeInterval t0, u (x,r) = q) ∧
    (∀ (k : ℕ) (x : WaveMap.Space n) (r : ℝ), 0 < k → x ∉ C →
      r ∈ WaveMap.timeInterior t0 → iteratedFDeriv ℝ k u (x,r) = 0) ∧
    (∀ r ∈ WaveMap.timeInterior t0, ∀ (j : Fin n) (f : WaveMap.Space n → ℝ),
      f ∈ WaveMap.terms u r j →
      Continuous f ∧ Function.support f ⊆ C ∧
      (∃ K : ℝ, 0 ≤ K ∧ ∀ x ∈ WaveMap.box n R, |f x| ≤ K) ∧
      Measurable f ∧ Integrable f) ∧
    (∀ x ∈ WaveMap.box n R, ∀ r ∈ Icc (t0 - η) (t0 + η), |WaveMap.densityTime u x r| ≤ B)", "explanation": "Full N002 with the same common witnesses. Prove smoothness of density and its actual time derivative; continuity, support, measurability, box bounds and absolute integrability for exactly the seven listed terms. The list quantifies every coordinate; H001 supplies a coordinate when extracting the coordinate-independent entries. Coordinate product volume gives (2R)^n and compactness of the box. Set η=t0/4 and bound |e_t| on the compact space-time box to choose B. Keep C,R,η,B jointly quantified. Apply this at an arbitrary positive time to export energy-density integrability separately.", "source_steps": [{"id": "S006", "text": "Choose R as in F002 and put Q=[−R,R]^n. It is compact and has finite Lebesgue measure |Q|=(2R)^n. By smoothness H002, finite sums and products of the derivatives occurring in e, e_t, u_t·u_tt, ∂_{x_j}u·∂_{x_j}u_t, u_t·∂_{x_jx_j}u, u_t·∂_{x_j}u and ∂_{x_j}(u_t·∂_{x_j}u) are continuous, and e,e_t are jointly smooth. At every fixed r∈int J each listed spatial function vanishes outside C by F003, and is bounded on Q by continuity on a compact set. Continuous real functions on ℝ^n are measurable; a bound on Q and zero values outside Q dominate their absolute values by a finite constant times the indicator of Q. That indicator is integrable with integral (2R)^n, so each function is absolutely integrable. This applies to every spatial coordinate j with 1≤j≤n, and to every r∈int J."}, {"id": "S007", "text": "Define η=t0/4>0. Then [t0−η,t0+η]=[3t0/4,5t0/4]⊂(t0/2,3t0/2)=int J. The continuous real function |e_t| on the compact nonempty set Q×[t0−η,t0+η] is bounded. Taking a nonnegative bound proves that there exists B≥0 with |e_t(x,r)|≤B for every x∈Q and every r in this closed time interval."}, {"id": "S008", "text": "Choose B from F004. The witnesses C,R,η,B and defined J,Q have all the asserted common-support, measure, regularity, integrability and bound properties by F001,F002,F003,F005,F004. In particular t0∈int J and e(·,t0) is integrable. Introduce the existential quantifiers over C,R,η,B before closing their scope; discharge L1 and universally quantify the arbitrary t0. This proves the detailed common-support assertion and, by the arbitrariness of the positive base time, integrability of e(·,t) for every t>0."}], "boundary": "Only listed inputs are project premises. The pinned Mathlib environment is freely available; no sibling proof is supplied.", "background_supplement_sha256": "227c935068aa7190a4378d88365ed43b5aef18c9f514dc9ed3d66e20d77f91ae", "objects": [], "representations": [], "bridges": [], "shared_inputs": [], "background_note_index": [{"id": "R002.BG002", "at": "/inference/argument/2/text", "kind": "mathematical", "fact": "For a smooth u:ℝ^n×(0,∞)→ℝ^m, all its coordinate derivatives are smooth; finite sums, products and inner products of these derivatives are smooth. In particular e and e_t are smooth, with e_t=2u_t·u_tt+2Σ_{j=1}^n ∂_{x_j}u·∂_{x_j}u_t. If u=q on the open set (ℝ^n\C)×int J, every positive-order mixed space/time derivative vanishes there. Each spatial function listed in F005 is continuous and has support contained in C for fixed r∈int J."}, {"id": "R002.BG003", "at": "/inference/argument/2/text", "kind": "mathematical", "fact": "For finite n and R>0, Q=[−R,R]^n is compact and Lebesgue measurable, with measure (2R)^n<∞. If a real continuous f on ℝ^n vanishes outside a closed C⊂Q, then f is bounded on Q, measurable and absolutely integrable: for some finite M≥0, |f|≤M 1_Q and ∫|f|≤M(2R)^n."}, {"id": "R002.BG004", "at": "/inference/argument/3/text", "kind": "mathematical", "fact": "With t0>0, η=t0/4 and Q=[−R,R]^n for R>0, K=Q×[t0−η,t0+η] is a nonempty compact subset of ℝ^n×(0,∞). If e_t is continuous on that domain, there exists B≥0 with |e_t|≤B on K."}]}}
